From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:23:35 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the USB1 controller, PHY and reset bank 1

Describe the second of the SoC's two DWC3 USB controllers and its PHY,
plus the RCU reset bank the PHY's reset line lives in. Everything is
disabled here; whether a USB port is populated, and how VBUS reaches
it, is a board property.

usb0 is omitted rather than declared and disabled. The vendor tree
disables it on every board in the GRX350 family (AVM 4.9
arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/avm/grx350_common.dtsi) because its pins are
not bonded out on any of them, and describing a controller nothing can
use, only to switch it off again everywhere, would be dead DT. Its
numbers are recorded in the comment so a board that does populate it
does not have to rediscover them.

The controller is a flattened snps,dwc3 node with no glue wrapper. The
vendor wraps it in a lantiq,dwc3-grx500 parent whose driver sets the
AXI endianness field in the chip-top syscon and then calls
of_platform_populate(); that field is now owned by the PHY driver, and
the dwc3 core binds snps,dwc3 from DT on its own and takes its own
optional clocks, resets and generic PHYs. The upstream direction is the
same one: CONFIG_USB_DWC3_GENERIC_PLAT's help text says platforms using
dwc3-of-simple can switch to it by flattening the dwc3 child node.

Dropping the wrapper also drops the "usb1" alias. The vendor glue calls
of_alias_get_id(node, "usb") and returns -ENODEV when the node is not
in /aliases, then uses the id only to interpolate into an error
message; nothing else in either tree reads it.

intel,syscon-offset names this controller's AXI configuration word in
the chip-top syscon at +0x34 (usb0's is +0x18). The vendor encodes the
same number as its glue node's own reg property and reads it back with
of_get_address() as a syscon offset, untranslated, on a node sitting
under a ranges-bearing bus -- a reg that is not an address. The
established idiom on this platform is a syscon phandle plus a named
driver-private property, as the PCIe root complexes already do with
intel,inbound-shift and intel,outbound-shift against the same syscon.

The PHY carries no interrupt. The vendor's GIC_SHARED 50 on this node
is over-current only, and there is no over-current path on this
hardware -- the pads that would feed it are the DECT and WLAN buttons,
which is why the vendor board files delete their own pinctrl_usb*
nodes outright.

rcu_reset1 is new. RCU_RST_REQ/RCU_RST_STAT at +0x10/+0x14 hold 32
lines and the USB PHY resets are not among them; they live in a second,
non-adjacent pair, request at +0x48 and status at +0x24, both already
inside this node's 0x100 window. The vendor needs no such node because
its reset driver (AVM 4.9 drivers/reset/reset-intel-syscon.c) presents
both banks as one controller with nr_resets = 64 and #reset-cells =
<1>, splitting the id itself as regidx = id >> 5 and regbit = id & 31.
Its <&rcu0 37> for this PHY is therefore bank 1 bit 5, which the
mainline lantiq,xrx200-reset binding spells <&rcu_reset1 5 5> -- the
request and status bit being the same position, as everywhere else on
this SoC.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index 604e523a6cd2..925890e8ee1e 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -82,6 +82,18 @@ rcu_reset0: reset-controller@10 {
 			#reset-cells = <2>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * Second reset bank. RCU_RST_REQ2 is at 0x48 and
+		 * RCU_RST_STAT2 at 0x24, so a vendor reset id of 5 translates
+		 * to <&rcu_reset1 5 5>.
+		 */
+		rcu_reset1: reset-controller@48 {
+			compatible = "lantiq,xrx200-reset";
+			reg = <0x48 0x4>,	/* RCU_RST_REQ2 */
+			      <0x24 0x4>;	/* RCU_RST_STAT2 */
+			#reset-cells = <2>;
+		};
+
 		/*
 		 * Restart handler. Bit 30 of RCU_RST_REQ triggers the SoC
 		 * reset, and the write must be a read-modify-write under the
@@ -337,4 +349,36 @@ pcie2: pcie@18e00000 {
 		intel,outbound-shift = <12>;
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Two Synopsys DesignWare USB3 DRD controllers, described as plain
+	 * snps,dwc3 nodes. The PHY driver owns the chip-top AXI endianness
+	 * word; intel,syscon's argument is that controller's AXI
+	 * configuration offset.
+	 */
+	usb1_phy: phy@1a600000 {
+		compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy";
+		reg = <0x1a600000 0x10000>;
+		#phy-cells = <0>;
+		clocks = <&cgu GCLK_USB1>;
+		resets = <&rcu_reset1 5 5>;
+		reset-names = "phy";
+		intel,syscon = <&chiptop>;
+		intel,syscon-offset = <0x34>;
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
+	usb1: usb@1a500000 {
+		compatible = "snps,dwc3";
+		reg = <0x1a500000 0x10000>;
+
+		interrupt-parent = <&gic>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SHARED 83 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+
+		dr_mode = "host";
+		snps,dis_rxdet_inp3_quirk;
+		phys = <&usb1_phy>;
+		phy-names = "usb3-phy";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
 };
